{
  "id": "COMPOUND:742 - DISEASE:52",
  "verbalization": "Clopidogrel, a widely used antithrombotic agent, appears to exert its effects through multiple indirect pathways that ultimately contribute to the development of Acute cholecystitis NOS. The evidence suggests that clopidogrel influences the regulation of nuclear pre-mRNA domain containing 1B, which in turn leads to Giant Lymph Node Hyperplasia and, subsequently, Acute cholecystitis NOS. This pathway highlights a potential role of clopidogrel in modulating cellular processes that may disrupt normal cholecystic function. Additionally, clopidogrel's interaction with ClpB homolog, mitochondrial AAA ATPase chaperonin, and PPFIA binding protein 1 also results in conditions that are linked to Acute cholecystitis NOS, indicating that the drug may exert its effects through diverse molecular mechanisms that are not fully understood. These findings suggest that clopidogrel may contribute to the pathogenesis of Acute cholecystitis NOS through multiple, interconnected pathways, emphasizing the complexity of its biological effects.",
